TEXT 144 -112 Left 3 ;#284 Where the parasitics set the band edges
TEXT 144 -48 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lxgpm-UXTNY
TEXT 144 0 Left 2 ;The same tee with the two parasitics from page 2
TEXT 144 32 Left 2 ;of Alan's notes: Lser in C1, Cpar across L1.
TEXT 144 624 Left 2 ;Alan lists the capacitor's self-resonance and the inductor's parasitic capacitance as what sets
TEXT 144 656 Left 2 ;the top of the band, and says the inductor is the worse of the two. Lser and Cpar are the
TEXT 144 688 Left 2 ;smallest models of each: 1 nH inside the capacitor, 10 pF across the inductor.
TEXT 144 736 Left 2 ;L1 and Cpar resonate at 1.59 MHz. At that frequency the pair is an open circuit, which is the
TEXT 144 768 Left 2 ;best a feed inductor can do. Above it the pair turns capacitive, its impedance falls with
TEXT 144 800 Left 2 ;frequency, and it starts shunting the signal into the supply. That closes the band at the top.
TEXT 144 848 Left 2 ;C1 and Lser resonate at 50.3 MHz. Below it the branch is capacitive, as intended; above it the
TEXT 144 880 Left 2 ;branch is inductive, but 1 nH is only 6 ohm at 1 GHz, so here the inductor is the limit.
TEXT 144 928 Left 2 ;Alan gives no values at all, so every number in this file is a choice. The two parasitics are
TEXT 144 960 Left 2 ;plausible for small parts, not measurements of the commercial bias tees he shows.
TEXT 1000 160 Left 2 !.ac dec 200 1k 1G
TEXT 1000 200 Left 2 !.meas ac vmid FIND mag(V(rf_plus_dc)) AT 10Meg
TEXT 1000 240 Left 2 !.meas ac flow WHEN mag(V(rf_plus_dc))=0.353553 RISE=1
TEXT 1000 280 Left 2 !.meas ac fhigh WHEN mag(V(rf_plus_dc))=0.353553 FALL=1
TEXT 1000 320 Left 2 !.meas ac vpeak MAX mag(V(rf_plus_dc))
